messages.reasoning_content:
Now I have a very clear picture of the existing domain model and tests. Let me now design the change.

## Design

**The rule**: On a `passed` verdict, before handing to "the agent the pull request names", ask whether that named request has been *answered by a push* — and if so, return control to whoever made the request.

The mechanics in `validate_pull_request.ts`:

1. Currently: `reviewerAgent = latestRequestedAgentOn(repo, number, isKnownAgent)` — the newest request in the shaped thread (comments newest-first, body fallback).

2. New: we need richer info about the newest request:
   - **Who asked** (person / agent X / the body).
   - **When** (created_at, or body → treated as the standing request).
   - **What it asks for** (the agent name).
   - Whether the newest ask is a dispatch marker (machinery take-up — not an ask).

3. And the head commit's timestamp to compare against.

The domain function needs to be pure and testable. Following the repo's pattern (domain/work/pr-validation.ts holds the truth table; the entrypoint does I/O), I'll extend `ValidationInput` with the new facts and keep `decideValidationOutcome` pure:

```ts
export interface ValidationInput {
  conclusion: string;
  reviewerAgent: string;      // the newest request's agent (as today)
  engineerAgent: string;      // origin-agent (as today)
  askedByPerson?: boolean;    // (as today; failed path)
  priorRetries?: number;
  deliverableProblems?: readonly string[];
  // NEW:
  /** The newest ASK (person command, agent directive, or the body line) and who made it. */
  newestRequest?: {
    /** "person" | "agent" | "body" */
    askedBy: "person" | "agent" | "body";
    /** The agent that made the ask (AGENT_TAG on its comment), when askedBy === "agent". */
    agent?: string;
    /** When the ask was written; undefined for the body (the standing request). */
    at?: string; // ISO timestamp
  };
  /** When the pull request's head commit was created, or undefined if unreadable. */
  headCommittedAt?: string;
}
```

Decision logic on the passed path:

```
if (passed) {
  // The newest ask was answered by the push that produced this head commit:
  // a fix request answered, so control returns to whoever asked.
  const answeredByPush = newestRequest !== undefined
    && newestRequest.askedBy !== "body"
    && newestRequest.at !== undefined
    && headCommittedAt !== undefined
    && headCommittedAt > newestRequest.at;
  if (answeredByPush) {
    if (newestRequest.askedBy === "agent" && newestRequest.agent) {
      return { verdict: "passed", ...handTo(newestRequest.agent), summary: `CI concluded ${normalised}. The push answered ${newestRequest.agent}'s request; returning to it.` };
    }
    // A person's request answered by the push: the person is owed the answer.
    return { verdict: "passed", summary: `CI concluded ${normalised}. The push answered the request; back to the person who asked.` };
  }
  return { verdict: "passed", ...handTo(reviewerAgent), summary: ... };
}
```

Wait — I need to think again about the "answered by push → return to asker" vs the case where the ask named the REVIEWER (e.g. a person's `/reviewer` command) and a push happened after the comment. Can that happen? A person's comment while ball is with an agent gets deleted by the guard. The ball is with an agent when... engineer working. Person comments `/reviewer` mid-engineer-run → deleted by guard → not in shaped thread. Person comments `/reviewer` when ball is with person (e.g., after engineer done, PR green) → kept, newest ask = person's `/reviewer`. Now a push after that comment? Only an agent could push, and no agent will run (ball with person, validation only dispatches on... hmm — wait: person comments `/reviewer` → manual-comment workflow → dispatchPrValidation(asked_by_person=true) → validation → CI green → my new rule: is the ask answered by a push? head commit vs comment time: the head commit was created BEFORE the comment (engineer's earlier push). So no. → dispatch reviewer (current behavior). ✓.

But hmm, one dangerous case: the person comments `/reviewer` and CI is currently RED (engineer's last push failed). Validation → CI red → askedByPerson=true → dispatch nobody, person notified. Person then... fine.

Another: what if between the person's comment and the validation read, the ENGINEER's leftover push lands? Can't be — the engineer's run already ended (ball was with person).

OK here's the remaining subtle scenario for "ask answered by push": the reviewer's directive `/engineer` (agent ask) — between the directive and validation, the engineer pushed. Head commit newer than directive → answered → return to reviewer (AGENT_TAG). ✓ THE FIX.

And: person's `/engineer` (person ask) → engineer pushes → validation → answered → return to person. ✓ per issue.

And: body `/reviewer` → never consumed. ✓ no regression.

But wait, what about the engineer's OWN second push after being re-dispatched by validation (the #211 wasted-run scenario, post-fix)? Post-fix flow: reviewer directive → engineer pushes → validation → reviewer dispatched (new turn). Reviewer reviews, satisfied, ends done → person. No more validation. ✓.

What if the reviewer is NOT satisfied and issues `/engineer` again → engineer pushes → validation → reviewer again. This loop is legitimate review iteration (each cycle = a new commit). The runner's progress guard (`CHANGED_TAG` consecutive-no counter) — the engineer's runs push, so "changed". Hmm, could reviewer+engineer ping-pong forever? Each cycle requires the engineer to make a change and the reviewer to still object. That's the nature of review; the issue's condition is "the SAME request must not cycle" — satisfied: one request → at most one engineer run + one reviewer run.

Hmm wait, actually let me re-check one thing — is it "one request → one engineer run"? The reviewer's directive dispatches the engineer via handoff (runner machinery, not validation). The engineer runs ONCE per directive. If CI fails after the engineer's push, validation dispatches the engineer AGAIN (failed path — retry loop, bounded by CI_RETRY_LIMIT). If CI passes → reviewer. So per directive: engineer runs (1 + ≤3 CI-fail retries) → reviewer. Bounded. ✓.

Now — the dispatch-marker question. In the #211 timeline, the newest REQUEST per `latestRequestedAgent` was the dispatch marker (`atomaton:dispatch=engineer`), not the reviewer's directive. Because the marker (written when the engineer was dispatched) is NEWER than the reviewer's directive comment. So "the newest ask" must SKIP dispatch markers to find the reviewer's directive underneath. My design says: the newest ask = newest comment that requests an agent and is not a dispatch marker.

Hmm wait, but hold on: is skipping markers right when the newest ask is a PERSON command and the marker for its dispatch is newer? Person comments `/engineer` → manual workflow → dispatchPrValidation → validation → green → dispatch engineer (answers-request=true — no marker written for validation dispatches! Look: `if (!d.answersRequest)` — answersRequest=true skips marker posting entirely). So for the person-command-on-PR flow, there's NO marker; the person's comment is the newest ask. ✓.

For the handoff flow (agent → agent on a PR): reviewer directive → the RUNNER machinery dispatches the engineer. How? The runner's handoff — via `dispatch_agent.ts`/`dispatchRunner` WITHOUT answers-request? The handoff is "making a request, not answering one" (per dispatch_agent.ts docs: "A hand-off does not set it"). So a marker IS posted for handoffs. So the thread after the reviewer's directive: [reviewer directive (handed-off event)], [marker dispatch=engineer (asked event)], [engineer result done (returned)], [push → validation].

Newest non-marker ask = reviewer's directive. ✓. head commit (push) newer than directive → answered → return to reviewer via AGENT_TAG on the directive comment. ✓.

But wait — hmm, now what about the very FIRST flow: create_pr → validation → green → dispatch reviewer with answers-request=true (no marker). So the body's `/reviewer` ask has no marker. Reviewer runs. Fine.

What about a RE-DISPATCH of the reviewer by validation in the fix loop: validation dispatches reviewer with answers-request=true → no marker → reviewer's run → directive `/engineer` → handoff WITH marker → engineer → push → validation → newest non-marker ask = the NEW reviewer directive (newer than the old one). ✓.

Edge: reviewer's run ends `done` (satisfied) after being dispatched by validation. Ball → person. ✓.

Now — the `askedBy` determination for the newest ask:
- If the comment `user.type === "Bot"` (i.e., not human) → machinery/agent comment. Then:
  - If it has DISPATCH_TAG → marker → skip (not an ask).
  - Else if it has a directive (parseCommentCommand.agent !== "") → an agent's ask. The asking agent = AGENT_TAG.read(body) — the runner tags result comments with `atomaton:agent=X`. What if AGENT_TAG is absent? Fallback: hmm. Older result comments? The AGENT_TAG exists ("used by reconcile_github_session.ts"). Is it written on every result comment? Let me check post_result_comment.ts to be sure. If absent, we can't identify the asker → fall back to current behavior (dispatch the asked-for agent)? That would reintroduce the bug for old-style comments, but all current comments carry the tag. Safer fallback: dispatch nobody → person? Hmm. Let me check how result comments are written.

- If the comment is human → person ask.
- If no comment ask → body fallback → "body" ask (never consumed).

Let me check post_result_comment.ts to confirm AGENT_TAG is on every result comment.

Also — the head commit timestamp: `pr.head.sha` → fetch the commit (`repos/{repo}/commits/{sha}`) → `commit.committer.date` (or `commit.author.date`). Committer date is when the commit was made. The push happens right after (seconds). The reviewer's directive comment is written BEFORE the engineer's run starts (the run starts after the directive's dispatch). So commit date > directive date in the normal flow. Clock skew: both timestamps are GitHub-side (comment created_at = GitHub server; commit committer.date = the runner's clock at commit time). GitHub-hosted runners sync clock well. A few seconds of skew could theoretically flip an ordering when the push happens <skew after the ask — but the push happens after a full engineer run (minutes), so skew is a non-issue in practice. And in the failure direction (commit appears older than the ask when it's actually newer) we'd just fall back to current behavior (dispatch the asked-for engineer) — the status quo. Acceptable.

Wait, actually — I realize I should double-check one thing: in the #211 flow, is the reviewer's directive comment really carrying a `/engineer` line that parseCommentCommand accepts? The reviewer ends its run with a directive line — the runner posts the result comment whose body ends with `/engineer`. Yes — that's the standard handoff (extract_directive reads the directive; `parseCommentCommand` reads the same syntax). ✓.

And AGENT_TAG on it: `atomaton:agent=reviewer`. Let me verify in post_result_comment.ts.

Now, where should the "who asked / when" reading live? The thread reading is in `adapters/github/thread.ts` (I/O) + `domain/work/thread.ts` (pure). I'll add:

In `domain/work/thread.ts`: a pure function that folds entries (with author info) into "the newest ask":

Hmm, the current `ThreadEntry` = { body, isHuman }. For this question I need timestamps and the AGENT_TAG. But timestamps aren't needed for the pure fold — the fold returns the newest ask with its author; the caller (validate_pull_request.ts) compares timestamps. Actually the fold needs the entries in order; the caller has them oldest-first with created_at. So:

```ts
// domain/work/thread.ts
/** Who made an ask, as the newest-ask fold reports it. */
export type AskAuthor =
  | { kind: "person" }
  | { kind: "agent"; agent: string }
  | { kind: "body" };

export interface NewestAsk {
  /** The agent the ask names. */
  agent: string;
  /** Who made it. */
  author: AskAuthor;
}

/**
 * The newest ask in a thread, for the question "has this request been answered".
 * ... a dispatch marker is the machinery TAKING UP a request, so it is skipped ...
 */
export function newestAsk(body: string, comments: readonly ThreadEntry[], readers: TurnReaders): NewestAsk | undefined
```

Wait — but the entries passed in are shaped already? `latestRequestedAgent` shapes internally (it takes raw comments and shapes them). I'll follow the same shape: take raw comments (body, isHuman), shape them, walk newest-first, skip dispatch markers, return the first with requestedAgent !== "". Body fallback → { kind: "body" }.

Hmm, but I need the AGENT_TAG from the comment body — that's an adapter concern (tags.ts). The domain readers already parse things via injected readers (TurnReaders). I could extend the readers with an `agentAuthor: (body) => string` reader... but TurnReaders is shared. Hmm. Alternatively, the asker-agent extraction can be another reader in the `known` overlay like `isKnownAgent` does in `latestRequestedAgentOn`. Let me think about the cleanest approach that fits the existing architecture:

Option: extend `TurnReaders` with a third function? No — TurnReaders is the turn vocabulary; adding "author" to it muddies it. But actually — hmm, the domain fold needs to know who wrote an agent's comment. The AGENT_TAG format is an adapter fact. The existing pattern: `domain/work/thread.ts` takes readers as arguments, so adapter-specific parsing stays in adapters. The AGENT_TAG reader is in adapters/github/tags.ts (pure string ops, but kept in adapters as "the ONE place each tag's wire format is defined").

Cleanest: `newestAsk` in domain takes a readers object that includes `askingAgent: (body: string) => string` (the agent that wrote this comment, "" if not tagged). `adapters/github/thread.ts` provides it from AGENT_TAG. Then:

```ts
// domain/work/thread.ts
export interface AskReaders extends TurnReaders {
  /** The agent that wrote this comment, when the machinery tagged it with one. */
  authoringAgent: (body: string) => string;
}

export type AskAuthor = { kind: "person" } | { kind: "agent"; agent: string } | { kind: "body" };

export interface NewestAsk {
  agent: string;          // the agent the ask names
  author: AskAuthor;      // who made it
}

export function newestAsk(body: string, comments: readonly ThreadEntry[], readers: AskReaders): NewestAsk | undefined {
  const { comments: kept } = shapedThread(comments, readers);
  for (let i = kept.length - 1; i >= 0; i -= 1) {
    const entry = kept[i]!;
    const agent = readers.requestedAgent(entry.body);
    if (agent === "") continue;
    if (readers.isDispatchMarker(entry.body)) continue; // a take-up, not an ask
    const authorAgent = readers.authoringAgent(entry.body);
    return {
      agent,
      author: entry.isHuman ? { kind: "person" } : authorAgent ? { kind: "agent", agent: authorAgent } : { kind: "body" },
    };
  }
  return { agent: readers.requestedAgent(body), author: { kind: "body" } };
}
```

Hmm wait — "kind: body" for an untagged machinery comment is odd. If an agent's comment lacks AGENT_TAG, treating it as "body" (never consumed) = current behavior = safe fallback. Actually let me use a distinct kind... or just treat unidentifiable as body-fallback semantics. Hmm, but that conflates two things. Let me think about what the CONSUMER needs:

The consumer (validation passed path) needs to know:
- Is this ask consumable-by-push? (person and agent asks are; body is not)
- If consumed, where does control go? (person → nobody; agent X → X; body → n/a)

An untagged machinery comment with a directive: what IS that? The machinery's own comments: failure reports (no directive), dispatch markers (skipped), "waiting for CI" notices (no directive). Result comments from the runner — always tagged with AGENT_TAG? Need to verify. If some machinery comment carries a directive but no AGENT_TAG, returning to "nobody" (treat as person? as body?) — safest: treat as not-consumable (fall back to current behavior: dispatch the asked-for agent). I'll model it as a fourth author kind... no — simpler: `authoringAgent` returns "" and I return `{ kind: "body" }`? That reads wrong ("body" it is not).

Alternative modeling: the author is `string | "person" | "body"`:
- `"person"` — a human wrote it.
- `"body"` — the PR body (the standing request).
- agent name — an agent wrote it (identified by tag).
- hmm, and unidentifiable machinery → treat as `"body"` (not consumable) with a comment explaining.

Actually — cleanest for the domain: make `newestAsk` return `undefined` when the newest ask cannot be attributed to a person or an agent, and let the caller fall back to `reviewerAgent` (current behavior). "Cannot be attributed" = the machinery wrote it without an authoring tag. That keeps `decideValidationOutcome`'s input simple: it just receives `answeredByPush?: { returnTo: "person" } | { returnTo: string } | ...` hmm.

Hmm, wait. Let me simplify the contract between entrypoint and domain. The domain function's job: given facts, decide. Let me give it exactly the facts it needs, pre-computed by the adapter:

```ts
export interface ValidationInput {
  ...
  /**
   * The request a push has answered, when there is one.
   * ... "the newest ask (a person's command, an agent's hand-off directive) that
   * the head commit postdates. Absent when the newest ask is the body's own
   * `/<agent>` line -- the standing request a push does not answer -- or when
   * the newest ask postdates the head commit."
   */
  answeredRequest?: {
    /** "person" when a person's command was answered; otherwise the agent that asked. */
    returnTo: "person" | string;  // hmm, string union with literal
  };
}
```

And in the passed branch:
```ts
if (passed) {
  const answered = input.answeredRequest;
  if (answered?.returnTo === "person") {
    return { verdict: "passed", summary: `CI concluded ${normalised}. The push answered the request, which a person made; nobody is dispatched.` };
  }
  if (answered) {
    return { verdict: "passed", ...handTo(answered.returnTo), summary: `CI concluded ${normalised}. The push answered ${answered.returnTo}'s request; returning to ${answered.returnTo}.` };
  }
  return { verdict: "passed", ...handTo(reviewerAgent), summary: `CI concluded ${normalised}.` };
}
```

Hmm wait, `returnTo: "person" | string` — the literal gets swallowed by string. Type-wise that's just string. Better: `returnTo: string` where "person" is a reserved value? Or `{ who: { kind: "person" } | { kind: "agent"; agent: string } }`. Let me follow the codebase's style — they use discriminated unions with `kind` (see `handoff.ts`: `{ kind: "reinvoke-origin-agent"; parentIssue; agent }`). So:

```ts
export type AnsweredRequest =
  | { kind: "person" }                 // a person asked; the answer is theirs
  | { kind: "agent"; agent: string };  // an agent asked; control returns to it
```

And `decideValidationOutcome` takes `answeredRequest?: AnsweredRequest`.

The adapter (`validate_pull_request.ts`) computes it:
1. Read the thread with timestamps: needs comments with `created_at` + `user.type` + body. Currently `listComments` returns Comment[] without timestamps. I'll extend the local read in validate_pull_request.ts — hmm, or extend adapters/github/thread.ts with a new exported function `latestAnswerableAskOn(repo, number, readers)` returning `{ agent, author, at }`... 

Wait — I also need the head commit's timestamp: `gh api repos/{repo}/commits/{sha} --jq .commit.committer.date`.

Let me now also double check: `latestRequestedAgentOn` is still needed for `reviewerAgent` (the fallback / the asked-for agent). Yes — keep it.

Now, the timestamp comparison: where? In the adapter (I/O facts) or in the domain (pure)? The comparison `headCommittedAt > askedAt` is pure string comparison of ISO 8601 (same format, Z-suffixed) — but to keep the domain pure and the truth table testable, pass BOTH facts in and let the domain compare:

```ts
answeredRequest computed in adapter = { returnTo } — adapter does the comparison.
```
vs
```ts
input.newestAsk = { author: ..., at: "..." } | undefined; input.headCommittedAt = "...";
```

Hmm. The comparison is trivial; the DECISION (consumed → return to asker) is the domain's. But the truth table tests would then need timestamps... which is fine and actually good for the contract test the issue asks for ("#211 の形（レビュワーが /engineer → engineer が push → CI 緑）を固定する契約テスト。次に起動されるのが reviewer であること。").

I'll have the adapter compute the facts and pass:
- `newestAsk?: { author: "person" | { agent: string }; at: string }` — hmm, TypeScript: `author: "person" | { agent: string }`. Or keep it as the AnsweredRequest shape + at.

Let me go with passing to the domain:

```ts
/** The newest ask in the thread that is not the body's own line. */
newestAsk?: {
  /** Who made it: a person, or the agent named here. */
  returnTo: "person" | string;  // ← no, avoid
};
```

OK let me just settle: 

```ts
export interface NewestAsk {
  /** Who made the ask, and who therefore judges its answer. */
  author: { kind: "person" } | { kind: "agent"; agent: string };
  /** When the ask was written, as GitHub reports it. */
  at: string;
}
```

in domain/work/thread.ts, with:

```ts
export function newestAsk(body, comments, readers: TurnReaders & { authoringAgent: (body: string) => string }): NewestAsk | undefined
```

and in adapters/github/thread.ts:

```ts
export function newestAskOn(repo, number, isKnownAgent): NewestAsk | undefined
```

using the existing readThread + AGENT_TAG reader + isKnownAgent filter (same as latestRequestedAgentOn's known overlay).

Then in validate_pull_request.ts:

```ts
const newest = newestAskOn(repo, number, isKnownAgent);  // undefined when the newest ask is the body's, or unattributable
const headCommittedAt = readCommitDate(repo, headSha);    // "" when unreadable
```

and pass into decideValidationOutcome:

```ts
answeredRequest:
  newest && headCommittedAt && headCommittedAt > newest.at
    ? newest.author   // { kind: "person" } | { kind: "agent"; agent }
    : undefined,
```

Hmm, wait. `isKnownAgent` filtering: in `latestRequestedAgentOn`, a name that fails isKnownAgent is SKIPPED (falls back to next request). Should newestAskOn apply the same filter? Yes — a typo'd directive shouldn't become the return target. Apply the same known overlay.

Hmm, one more design question: should the newest-ask scan skip dispatch markers ONLY, or also skip... let me enumerate the comment types in a PR thread and how each reads:

1. PR body: `/<agent>` line + origin-agent tag. → fallback ask (kind: body).
2. Person's `/engineer` comment → ask by person. (Kept by shaping only if ball was with person.)
3. Person's ordinary comment → not an ask (requestedAgent=""), skipped by the scan. Also: ordinary comments do NOT get removed by the guard when ball is with person... but they might exist mid-thread. The scan just skips them. ✓.
4. Machinery's dispatch marker `atomaton:dispatch=X` → skip (take-up).
5. Runner's result comment: `atomaton:agent=X`, `atomaton:ended=...`, maybe directive `/Y` at the end.
   - With directive → ask by agent X (the asker!). ✓.
   - Without directive → not an ask.
6. Machinery failure comments (ci-retry) → `llm-context=include`, no directive → skipped.
7. Machinery "waiting for CI" notice → no directive → skipped.
8. `notify_unattended` comment → hmm, does it carry a directive? It's "nobody was dispatched, a person is told". No directive. Skipped.
9. dispatchPostMergeAgent's trigger comment: LLM_CONTEXT_TAG + prose. No directive. Skipped.

What about `parseCommentCommand` matching a `/stop`? `isControlCommand` — parse returns control ≠ agent → agent="" → skipped. ✓.

Now — the `atomaton:agent` tag on result comments: need to confirm it's written on EVERY result comment. Let me check post_result_comment.ts.

Also, one more important consideration: **the guard's shaping and the person's command**. A person's `/engineer` command on a PR arrives while ball is with... person (after reviewer done) → kept. While ball with agent → removed. Fine.

And: what about a person's command that validation is CURRENTLY answering (the manual-comment flow)? Person comments `/engineer` → validation dispatched. During validation, ball = agent's turn? The person's comment is an "asked" event → whoseTurn = agent. Validation runs, CI green → newest ask = person's `/engineer`, head commit OLDER (no push since) → NOT consumed → dispatch engineer (current behavior, answers-request=true). ✓ unchanged.

Then engineer pushes → validation #2 → newest ask = person's `/engineer` (still the newest ask — the engineer's run result comment has no directive), head commit NEWER → consumed, author person → return to person (dispatch nobody, notify step mentions... wait — WHO does notify_unattended mention? Let me check notify_unattended.ts — it must resolve the person from the thread. If it mentions the right person, ✓).

Hmm wait, actually — there's a subtlety: in validation #2, is the person's `/engineer` comment still the newest ask? Between validation #1's dispatch of the engineer and the engineer's push, the engineer ran and ended with a done result comment (no directive). So newest ask = person's `/engineer`. ✓. But ALSO — what if the engineer ends with a directive `/reviewer` (hands to reviewer itself)? Then: engineer ran, pushed, ended `/reviewer` → newest ask = engineer's directive naming reviewer, head commit newer than the PERSON's comment but is it newer than the engineer's directive? The directive comes after the push → head commit OLDER than the directive → not consumed → dispatch reviewer. ✓ — the engineer explicitly handed to the reviewer, which supersedes the person's ask. Good, the timestamp comparison handles that naturally.

Now let me also think: **is `headCommittedAt > newest.at` the right comparison, or should it be `>=`?** Commit dates and comment timestamps have second precision. A push immediately after a directive? Impossible (an engineer run takes minutes). But create_pr → validation: body ask is not consumed, no comparison. Person command → no push. I'll use `>` (strictly newer). Hmm — but consider equal timestamps (same second): treat as not-answered → current behavior. Safe.

Also — should I compare against ALL commits or just the head? The head is the newest commit. If head > ask, some push happened after the ask. If head < ask, could an EARLIER commit still postdate the ask? No — head is the newest. Hmm, wait: committer dates are not guaranteed monotonic across commits (rebases, amended commits can carry older dates). `commit_and_push` creates the commit with the current clock at commit time, then pushes. The head commit is the last one created. A rebase by a person could reorder dates, but agent flows don't rebase. Acceptable.

Actually — hmm, one more consideration. There's `pushed_at` on the PR (`gh pr view --json pushedAt`?) — hmm, the push time of the HEAD would be even more accurate ("the push that produced this validation"). But after force-push... no force pushes here. Actually `pull_request` objects don't expose pushed_at; the head commit's committer date is the available proxy. There's also the "timeline" but that's heavier. Committer date it is. I'll note the limitation in a comment.

Hmm, actually wait. Let me reconsider using the COMMIT date vs comparing against the VALIDATION dispatch time. The validation run starts right after the push. If headCommittedAt < validation started-at... no, that doesn't tell us whether the push answered the ASK. The ask must predate the push. Commit date is the right proxy for the push. OK.

But here's a subtlety: the commit date is when `git commit` ran — which could be BEFORE the ask! Scenario: engineer run in progress, has committed locally (but not pushed), reviewer's directive... no — the reviewer's directive comes from a COMPLETED reviewer run, and the engineer run that answers it starts after. The engineer commits within its run → after the directive. But: what if the engineer committed in a PREVIOUS run (before the directive) and pushes in the run after the directive? E.g., engineer run 1: commits but fails to push (crash). Reviewer reviews the OLD head, issues `/engineer` ("push your work"). Engineer run 2: pushes the EXISTING commit (committer date = run 1, BEFORE the directive). Validation: headCommittedAt < directive → not consumed → dispatch engineer AGAIN. Hmm! That's the "already up to date" case — the engineer re-runs, sees everything pushed... wait, would it even re-push? `git push` says "Everything up-to-date", moved=false... hmm, actually committed=false, pushed=false → moved=false → NO validation dispatch. So validation wouldn't even run. And the run ends... this is a corner case; the outcome (engineer re-runs once) is the status quo. Acceptable.

